# Divider / Horizontal Rule

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A divider is a thin line that separates one group of content from the next. In an entities card it separates groups of rows, such as lights from sensors. Use it sparingly. A heading or a gap often groups content better than a line.

Home Assistant has no element named `ha-divider`. It has several real dividers, and only one of them, the divider row, can be written by a dashboard author. This page owns all of them.

This page covers:

- Divider / Horizontal Rule
  - `<hr>`
  - `ha-divider`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A divider is a single line across the width of its container. It has a colour, a thickness and space above and below. It has no text.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hr"><code>&lt;hr&gt;</code></h3>

The HTML horizontal rule: a break between paragraph-level content. It is a void element with no content. Browsers expose it as a separator, and it cannot receive focus. Use CSS for its look, not the old `align`, `noshade`, `size` and `width` attributes. Do not use it to add space.

Home Assistant uses a plain rule inside some dialogs, such as the update dialog and the sun dialog. A dashboard author cannot write it.

<h3 id="child-ha-divider"><code>ha-divider</code></h3>

Home Assistant does not use the name `ha-divider`. It has these real dividers instead:

- **Divider row.** An entities card row of `type: divider`. This is the one a dashboard author can use. It is a line one pixel high. Its colour is `--entities-divider-color`, or `divider-color` when that is not set. The `style` option sets CSS on the line, for example its height or margin. The divider row has no role, so a screen reader does not announce it as a separator.
- **Web Awesome divider.** Home Assistant uses `wa-divider` inside its own screens, such as the date picker, an icon overflow menu and the settings table layout. A dashboard author cannot place it, and its settings are Web Awesome’s, not Home Assistant’s.
- **Form divider.** A form’s divider draws a plain rule with a one pixel top border in the quiet border colour.

```yaml
type: entities
entities:
  - light.kitchen_ceiling
  - type: divider
  - sensor.kitchen_temperature
```

The Civica theme sets these colours, which a divider takes: `divider-color` and the quiet border colour are `#CECECE` in light mode and `#50565A` in dark mode. `entities-divider-color` is not set, so the divider row follows `divider-color`. The darker interactive border, `#6F777B` in light mode and `#9BA4AA` in dark mode, is for controls, not dividers.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A divider row is native and takes the theme’s line colour in both modes.
- A divider is a quiet cue. The colours are light on purpose, so a line must never be the only way to tell groups apart.
- The divider row is not announced to a screen reader. Put the grouping in a heading or a label as well.
- You cannot restyle the Web Awesome divider. Test its look on the screen where you see it.
- Too many lines make a card look busy.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a divider between groups of equal weight, and group first with headings, lists and spacing. Use one divider where you would otherwise run groups together, not between every row. Do not use a line to add space.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: use headings or lists for structure, not a line alone.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: a line colour must not carry meaning.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: applies if the line conveys information. A decorative line need not meet it, so keep the information in text.

Fewer lines, with clear headings, make a card easier to read.

